.nh
.TH "PVEFORGE" "1" "Sep 2026" "pveforge" "pveforge manual"

.SH NAME
pveforge-user-ensure - Create a PVE user, or bring it to the state asked for


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBpveforge user ensure <target-id> <userid> [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a PVE user, or bring it to the state asked for. Idempotent: a user
already as asked is left alone, and root is never connected to.

.PP
The write runs as root over SSH with pveum, never with the roster's API token,
which must never hold User.Modify. The read that decides whether to write uses
the token, and root's pveum when the token may not read users.

.PP
--enable and --disable set the user's state; with neither, a new user is
enabled and an existing one keeps its state. --comment and --email are set
when given. --group adds the user to a group (repeatable); membership is only
ever added, never removed. A @pve user is created with no password: it cannot
log in until one is set with pveum passwd. A @pam user also needs an account on
the node, which PVE does not create.

.PP
Joining a group grants the group's ACLs, so --group is guarded like acl grant:
  - the user that owns the roster's own token may join no group (always; there
    is no override);
  - no user may join a group that holds, on any path, a role conferring
    an escalating privilege (Permissions.Modify, User.Modify, Sys.Modify, Realm.Allocate, Sys.Console, VM.Monitor, Realm.AllocateUser, Datastore.Allocate, Mapping.Modify), unless
    --allow-escalating-role is given (read as root before the write).
Allowed, each escalating holding of each group joined is warned about.

.PP
These checks hold only for the groups, ACLs and roles as read just before the
write. A group granted an escalating role, or a role widened (pveum role
modify), after the join is not caught: the user then holds it unwarned.
Nothing serialises user ensure against acl grant, or either against changes
made outside pveforge.

.PP
Disabling the user that owns the roster's own token is refused: it would lock
pveforge out of the target.

.PP
If the change is made but its result cannot be re-read afterwards, or is
re-read without every requested value, a one-line warning is printed on
stderr; stdout is unchanged and the exit status is still 0.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB--allow-escalating-role\fP[=false]
	allow joining a group that holds a role conferring an escalating privilege: Permissions.Modify, User.Modify, Sys.Modify, Realm.Allocate, Sys.Console, VM.Monitor, Realm.AllocateUser, Datastore.Allocate, Mapping.Modify

.PP
\fB--comment\fP=""
	set the user's comment

.PP
\fB--disable\fP[=false]
	disable the user: it can no longer log in or use its API tokens

.PP
\fB--email\fP=""
	set the user's email

.PP
\fB--enable\fP[=false]
	enable the user

.PP
\fB--group\fP=[]
	add the user to this group (repeatable; membership is only added)

.PP
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for ensure

.PP
\fB--host-key-fingerprint\fP=""
	the target's SSH host key fingerprint as you verified it, SHA256:<base64> exactly as ssh-keygen -l -E sha256 prints it: for a target holding no pin, the --no-ssh-key password connection must present that key, checked before the password is sent, instead of trusting the host key on first use; for a target holding a pin it must equal that pin, or nothing is dialed. It must be the fingerprint of the key type pveforge's SSH client negotiates: ECDSA when the host serves one (PVE does by default), so pinning the host's ED25519 key is refused there (fail-closed); ssh-keyscan and ssh-keygen -lf list every type the host serves.

.PP
\fB--lock-wait\fP=0s
	how long to wait for another pveforge process's lock on this object before giving up (0 = the 15m0s default; at most 1h0m0s). The default exceeds the 10m0s ceiling on waiting for a PVE task because a legitimate lock holder may be in such a wait the whole time

.PP
\fB--no-ssh-key\fP[=false]
	connect as root with the PVE password (PVEFORGE_PVE_PASSWORD, else a prompt) for this run only, as bootstrap --no-ssh-key does; the connection is checked against the target's stored host key pin, else --host-key-fingerprint, else the host key is trusted on first use; required for a target that holds no SSH key

.PP
\fB--roster\fP=""
	path to the roster file (overrides PVEFORGE_ROSTER and the default ./pveforge.toml)


.SH SEE ALSO
\fBpveforge-user(1)\fP
